  "answer": "**Final status: passing.** On the second run, build, unit and component all passed (run `20261021T111618Z-37d304`). It took one fix.\n\n**What failed:** The first run passed the build, then failed at the unit stage (exit code 2), so the component stage was skipped. About 40 tests failed in `SemanticMatcherTests` and `ResultMatcherTests`, on both net9.0 and net10.0. They all threw `ArgumentException: Unknown semantic matcher 'isnull' for field 'deletedAt'`, or the same error for `isuuid`, `isnotnull` or `greaterthan`.\n\n**Root cause:** In `src/ConfIT/Matching/SemanticMatcher.cs`, `ParseSpec` converted matcher names to lowercase. But the `BuiltIns` dictionary uses camelCase keys (`isUuid`, `greaterThan`) and compares them case-sensitively. As a result, no built-in matcher name could ever be found, and custom names like `isDomainId` couldn't either.\n\n**What was changed** (all in `src/ConfIT/Matching/SemanticMatcher.cs`):\n- **Line 9:** `BuiltIns` now uses `StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase`, so built-in names match whatever case is written.\n- **Lines 85 and 91:** `ParseSpec` keeps the matcher name as written instead of lowercasing it.\n\n**Side effects of the fix:**\n- Custom matchers are now looked up by the exact name the user wrote, so they are case-sensitive.\n- The check for custom names that clash with built-in names now ignores case.\n\nI didn't run anything or edit any files myself; the subagents did all of it. Nothing was committed.",
